Short answer: Yes — whether Selenium opens a visible browser when you run selected pytest tests in a Jenkins pipeline depends on how the tests and Jenkins agent are configured: by default Selenium will try to launch a real browser process, but in CI you typically run browsers in headless mode or on a remote display (Xvfb) or a remote WebDriver (Selenium Grid / browser container), so no visible window appears on the Jenkins host unless you explicitly provide a GUI/display.

How Selenium is launched in pytest

Local WebDriver call: If your pytest test constructs a WebDriver (e.g., webdriver.Chrome()/webdriver.Firefox()) without headless options and the Jenkins agent has a desktop/display session, Selenium will start a real browser process and it will attempt to open a visible window on that display.

pytest-selenium plugin: Using pytest-selenium or fixtures that create a selenium WebDriver will also start a browser per test (or per fixture scope) as configured; the plugin will follow whatever browser options you pass (local, headless, or remote).

Why Jenkins often does NOT show a browser window

Headless mode: In CI you typically pass browser options to run Chrome/Firefox in headless mode (e.g., ChromeOptions().add_argument("--headless") or options.headless = True). Headless runs the browser without opening a visible UI; tests still drive the browser but nothing appears on the screen. This is the common recommended approach for pipelines.

No desktop session: Jenkins agents often run as background services (no X server / no logged-in desktop). Even if the browser process starts, there is no visible graphical session, so you won’t see windows. In that case tests will fail unless you provide a headless option or virtual display.

Virtual/Xvfb or containers: Alternatives to headless mode include running an X virtual framebuffer (Xvfb) on the agent or running tests inside containers that provide a virtual display or use a Selenium Grid / remote browser node. Those let a non-headed environment host UI browsers without a physical display.

Headed vs Headless — in depth

Headed (visible) mode:

Behavior: Browser launches with UI and all DevTools/graphics; you can see the browser interacting with the page. Works the same as local developer runs.

Requirements: A running GUI session and permissions for the Jenkins user to open windows, or a forwarded display (e.g., SSH X11 forwarding).

Pros: Easier to debug visually, useful when debugging flaky tests.

Cons: Not suitable for typical CI agents, slower, requires more system resources, and often impossible when Jenkins runs as a daemon without a display.

Headless mode:

Behavior: Browser runs without any visible UI; the WebDriver API still controls it and tests run normally. For Chrome and Firefox this is a supported mode via options/flags.

How to enable: Example for Chrome in Python:

from selenium import webdriver

options = webdriver.ChromeOptions(); options.add_argument("--headless") or options.headless = True

driver = webdriver.Chrome(options=options)

Pros: Fast, resource-efficient, works on CI agents without GUI, stable for automated runs.

Cons: Some visual or layout issues may differ slightly from headed, and certain features relying on GPU or media may behave differently.

Alternatives to headless if you need a GUI on CI

Xvfb (virtual display): Start Xvfb on the Jenkins agent and set DISPLAY so browser windows render into the virtual framebuffer; you can capture screenshots or run visual debugging without a physical display.

Selenium Grid / remote WebDriver: Run tests against remote browser nodes (Docker containers, cloud providers, or Grid) — Jenkins only sends WebDriver commands; the browser runs elsewhere (no local windows). This is scalable and keeps CI agents lightweight.

CI container images with browsers: Use Docker images that include browsers and virtual displays (or use VNC-enabled images) so you can reproduce an environment similar to local machines.

Practical checklist for your Jenkins + pytest + Selenium job

Decide whether you want visible UI: usually use headless in CI unless debugging.

If headless: add headless flags to browser options in your pytest fixtures or configuration.

If headed-on-remote: configure Xvfb or use container/grid that exposes display/VNC.

If using pytest-selenium plugin: configure browser via plugin options or fixtures so tests pick up headless/remote settings.

Ensure Jenkins agent has browser binaries and compatible WebDriver (chromedriver/geckodriver) in PATH, or use WebDriverManager/container management so tests can start browsers reliably.

