Direct answer: I’ll give you a ready-to-drop pytest fixture (conftest.py) that starts Chrome in robust headless mode for CI, plus a Jenkinsfile stage that runs selected pytest tests inside a Docker agent with browsers installed (so no visible UI). Each code block below is explained briefly.

conftest.py (headless Chrome fixture)

This fixture creates a Chrome WebDriver with common CI-safe options (--headless, --no-sandbox, window-size, disable-gpu, disable-dev-shm-usage). Put this file at your tests root so all tests can use the driver by declaring the fixture name.

python
# conftest.py
import pytest
from selenium import webdriver
from selenium.webdriver.chrome.service import Service as ChromeService
from selenium.webdriver.chrome.options import Options
from webdriver_manager.chrome import ChromeDriverManager

@pytest.fixture(scope="function")
def chrome_driver():
    options = Options()
    options.add_argument("--headless=new")  # headless mode (use --headless for older chrome)
    options.add_argument("--no-sandbox")
    options.add_argument("--disable-dev-shm-usage")
    options.add_argument("--disable-gpu")
    options.add_argument("--window-size=1366,1050")
    options.add_argument("--remote-allow-origins=*")  # if needed for newer chrome versions
    options.add_argument("--disable-extensions")
    # optional: set user-agent or other flags if your app checks headers
    # options.add_argument("user-agent=My-CI-Agent/1.0")

    service = ChromeService(ChromeDriverManager().install())
    driver = webdriver.Chrome(service=service, options=options)
    yield driver
    driver.quit()
Explanation: This uses webdriver-manager to auto-install a compatible chromedriver and returns a fresh driver per test; it quits after each test to avoid resource leaks. You can change scope to "session" if you want one browser per test session but be mindful of test isolation.

