How it works and why to use it

Base: python:3.11-slim keeps image small while providing Python runtime.

Chrome: installs google-chrome-stable from Google's apt repo so Chrome is up-to-date and compatible.

Chromedriver: fetches the chromedriver matching the major/minor Chrome version; this avoids compatibility issues. If you prefer pinning, set CHROMEDRIVER_VERSION in build args.

Dependencies: adds necessary libraries Chrome requires in Linux containers to run headless.

Headless: tests should still pass headless flags from your conftest.py fixture; the container provides the browser binaries and driver so Jenkins doesn't need public selenium images.

Build and usage examples

Build (from repo root):

docker build -t myorg/python-chrome:3.11 .

Run tests locally:

docker run --rm -v "$(pwd)":/app myorg/python-chrome:3.11 pytest tests/ -q

CI: push image to your registry, use in Jenkinsfile docker agent or Kubernetes pod.

Optional improvements and notes

Use build args to pin Chrome/Chromedriver versions:

docker build --build-arg CHROMEDRIVER_VERSION=121.0.6167.85 -t myorg/python-chrome:121 .

Avoid running Chrome as root in production images; for CI it's often acceptable but you can add a non-root user and adjust permissions.

If /dev/shm is small in CI containers you may see crashes; either add --shm-size=1g to docker run or use Chrome option --disable-dev-shm-usage (already in your fixture).

If you prefer smaller images, consider installing Chromium from deb rather than google-chrome-stable, but Chrome stable is usually more consistent for tests.

For ARM builds (Apple Silicon runners), you'd need multi-arch handling or use chromium builds that support arm64.

