Yes — for a smaller image and ARM/AMD support, the best practical pattern is: use a multi-stage Dockerfile, install Chrome and ChromeDriver in a build/runtime stage that matches the target platform, and publish with docker buildx build --platform linux/amd64,linux/arm64. Docker Buildx is the standard way to produce multi-architecture images from one Dockerfile.

Recommended approach
For Selenium tests, the cleanest setup is usually:

Python base image in the final runtime stage.

Install only runtime browser dependencies there.

Copy in your app code and install Python packages without cache.

Build and push a multi-arch manifest with Buildx.

For Chrome specifically, one important caveat is that multi-arch support is easier with Chromium on Debian-based images; Chrome stable can still work, but you must ensure the browser and driver match the target architecture. Multi-arch Selenium images are now available for Selenium Grid, but since you want your own minimal image, Buildx is the right tool.

Multi-stage Dockerfile
This version keeps the final image smaller by separating dependency build from runtime, and it supports both linux/amd64 and linux/arm64 when built with Buildx. It uses python:3.12-slim and installs Chrome/ChromeDriver in the runtime stage.

text
# syntax=docker/dockerfile:1.7

FROM python:3.12-slim AS base

ENV DEBIAN_FRONTEND=noninteractive \
    PIP_NO_CACHE_DIR=1 \
    PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1 \
    PYTHONUNBUFFERED=1

RUN apt-get update && apt-get install -y --no-install-recommends \
    ca-certificates \
    curl \
    gnupg \
    unzip \
    wget \
    fonts-liberation \
    libasound2 \
    libatk-bridge2.0-0 \
    libatk1.0-0 \
    libc6 \
    libcairo2 \
    libcups2 \
    libdbus-1-3 \
    libdrm2 \
    libgbm1 \
    libgdk-pixbuf2.0-0 \
    libnspr4 \
    libnss3 \
    libx11-6 \
    libx11-xcb1 \
    libxcomposite1 \
    libxdamage1 \
    libxext6 \
    libxfixes3 \
    libxrandr2 \
    xdg-utils \
 && rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/*

FROM base AS python-deps

WORKDIR /build
COPY requirements.txt .
RUN pip install --upgrade pip && pip wheel --wheel-dir=/wheels -r requirements.txt

FROM base AS runtime

WORKDIR /app

RUN set -eux; \
    wget -q -O /tmp/google.pub https://dl.google.com/linux/linux_signing_key.pub; \
    gpg --dearmor -o /usr/share/keyrings/google-linux.gpg /tmp/google.pub; \
    echo "deb [arch=amd64,arm64 signed-by=/usr/share/keyrings/google-linux.gpg] http://dl.google.com/linux/chrome/deb/ stable main" > /etc/apt/sources.list.d/google-chrome.list; \
    apt-get update; \
    if [ "$(dpkg --print-architecture)" = "amd64" ]; then \
      apt-get install -y --no-install-recommends google-chrome-stable; \
    else \
      apt-get install -y --no-install-recommends chromium chromium-driver; \
    fi; \
    rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/* /tmp/google.pub

COPY --from=python-deps /wheels /wheels
COPY requirements.txt .
RUN pip install --no-index --find-links=/wheels -r requirements.txt && rm -rf /wheels

COPY . .

ENV DISPLAY=:99

CMD ["pytest", "-q"]
Why this is smaller
The first stage builds Python wheels once, then the runtime stage installs them from local wheel files instead of re-downloading and rebuilding everything. Multi-stage builds are a standard way to separate build-time and run-time dependencies, which reduces final image size and attack surface.

This Dockerfile also avoids keeping apt caches, temporary files, and build-only tooling in the final image. That matters more once you start adding browser dependencies, because browser images get large quickly.

Buildx commands
Build and push a multi-arch image like this:

bash
docker buildx create --use --name multiarch-builder
docker buildx build \
  --platform linux/amd64,linux/arm64 \
  -t yourname/python-selenium:latest \
  --push .
If you only want a local test build on your machine:

bash
docker buildx build --platform linux/amd64 -t python-selenium:test --load .
Buildx is designed for publishing a single image tag that resolves to the correct architecture automatically at pull time.

